.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_WRITE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_write \- append bytes to the response body
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_write(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                   const void *data, size_t len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends len bytes of data to the internal response body buffer.  The
buffer grows automatically using an overflow-safe doubling strategy:
capacity is at least doubled on each reallocation and the computation
is guarded against size_t wrap-around.  ccol_not_enough_memory is
returned both when len would overflow size_t when added to the current
body length and when the underlying allocator returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if resp is NULL, or if data is NULL and len > 0.
ccol_not_enough_memory if the body length would overflow size_t or if
                       the allocator cannot satisfy the request.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
For a request whose method is CHTTP_HEAD, the server reports the real
accumulated body length via the Content-Length response header (the
same value a GET would report, per RFC 7231 4.3.2) but never writes the
actual body bytes to the wire.  A handler reachable via CHTTP_HEAD
(explicitly, or through a CHTTP_ANY registration) therefore does not
need to special-case the method before calling this function.

The same suppression applies, independently of the request method,
whenever the response's final status code is a 1xx (informational),
204, or 304: RFC 9110 6.4.1/15.2.1/15.4.5 forbid a body on any of the
three, so bytes written via this function are silently withheld from
the wire for those statuses too.  A 1xx or 204 additionally never
carries an auto-injected Content-Length header, unlike HEAD/304, where
one matching the written body length is still reported.  See
chttpsvr_resp_set_status(3).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write_str (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_printf (3),
